**Theorem 7.4 (a conditional nine-low obstruction).** Suppose, in
addition, that every group is a singleton and every set $I$ of
at most eight endpoint columns for which
$A_Iw=B$ has a solution strictly positive on $I$ is linearly
independent. Then $U_8$ is the finite set of vertex contrasts.
If also $r_-<r_+$, every other contrast in that interval has
$n_t(r)=9$. These additional hypotheses are a conditional
obstruction, not an assertion about the original source.

Proof. An edge with at most eight groups would have at most eight
positive endpoint coordinates in its relative interior, because
the groups are singletons. Those columns represent $B$ with
strictly positive weights and are independent by hypothesis.
Equation (FC.20) would give dimension zero, contrary to its being
an edge. Thus no edge is retained in $\mathcal H_8$. All vertices
are retained by Theorem 6.1, giving exactly their finite contrast
set. A nontrivial interval has points outside that set; (FC.17)
gives nine low at each such point. Nothing here verifies the
additional independence or nonconstant-interval hypotheses for
an actual original attained tuple. $\square$

